The classifier weights downloaded from https://huggingface.co/AtharvIITB/meow-decoder carry the licence stated
on that page: beats.npz follows the Earth Species Project encoder (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, non-commercial);
ast.npz follows CatMeows (CC BY 4.0).

Two small models ship inside the package:

pitch.npz      Situation guess from pitch-contour shape, for cats nobody has taught yet. Trained on CatMeows
    (CC BY 4.0, credits above) by benchmark/pitch.py. The approach follows the Meowsic project's finding that
    food-related meows tend to rise in pitch and vet-related meows to fall: S. Schötz, J. van de Weijer,
    "A Study of Human Perception of Intonation in Domestic Cat Meows", Speech Prosody 7, 2014.

detectors.npz  Hissing and yowling detectors on the BEATs embedding, used only with the default (non-commercial)
    BEATs encoder. Trained by benchmark/detectors.py on:
      FSD50K (E. Fonseca et al., "FSD50K: An Open Dataset of Human-Labeled Sound Events", IEEE/ACM TASLP 2022,
      https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4060432): cat clips and a sample of other sounds. Each clip keeps its own
      Freesound licence (CC0, CC BY, CC BY-NC or Sampling+), listed in FSD50K's metadata;
      the Freesound recordings listed in benchmark/detector_labels.csv (CC0 or CC BY; uploaders named there);
      CatMeows (CC BY 4.0).
    Because some FSD50K clips are CC BY-NC, treat detectors.npz as non-commercial, like the BEATs encoder it uses.

detectors_ast.npz  Yowl detector for AST mode, on AudioSet's 527 scores. Trained by benchmark/detectors.py
    --encoder ast only on clips licensed CC0 or CC BY: FSD50K clips with those licences (each clip's licence and
    uploader are in FSD50K's metadata, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.4060432), the Freesound recordings in
    benchmark/detector_labels.csv, and CatMeows (CC BY 4.0). CC BY requires attribution: this notice, FSD50K's
    metadata and detector_labels.csv provide it.
